This is actually the "Home Stretch" error! It means your code successfully built, but Vercel is looking in a folder called build while Vite (your framework) put the finished files in a folder called dist.

We just need to sync them up one last time.

The Final Configuration
Go back to your Settings > General (use the Desktop Mode trick to see the "Save" button) and set these exact three values:

Build Command: npm run build (You can remove the || true now since it's clearly building successfully!).

Output Directory: Toggle Override to ON and type: dist

Install Command: Keep it as npm install --legacy-peer-deps.

Why "dist"?
Most modern apps (Vite/Lovable) create a folder named dist (short for distribution) when they are finished. Your last error showed that Vercel looked for build and found nothing. Switching this to dist is the final "handshake."

One Last Check in GitHub
If the build still fails after changing the setting to dist, it might be because of that || true trick we used in the package.json earlier. If the build "fails" but pretends to succeed, it doesn't actually create the files.

If changing the setting to dist doesn't work:

Go to GitHub -> frontend/package.json.

Change "build": "vite build || true" back to just "build": "vite build".

Commit and Redeploy.

Summary: 99% of the time, changing the Output Directory in Vercel settings to dist is what fixes this specific "No Output Directory" error. You are seconds away from having a live link!

Command "npm run build " exited with 127

We are playing "Whack-a-Mole" with these error codes, but 127 is actually very specific. It means "Command Not Found."

Vercel is trying to run npm run build, but because of that "Services" preset and the folder confusion, it can't even find the npm tool or your package.json anymore.

The "Clean Slate" Solution
Since we've tried a few different settings and GitHub edits, the configuration has likely become a "knot" that's hard to untie. Let’s cut the knot.

1. Change the Framework Preset (The big one)
In your Project Settings > General:

Tap that "Services" dropdown.

Change it to Vite.

Why: "Services" is for complex backend/frontend splits. Vite is exactly what your bike tracker uses. It will automatically set the Build and Output settings to the correct defaults.

2. Reset the Build Commands
Once you switch to Vite, set these and Save:

Build Command: npm run build (No || true this time, let's see the real result).

Output Directory: dist (Vite will fill this in automatically).

Install Command: npm install --legacy-peer-deps (Keep this!).

3. The GitHub Cleanup
Go to GitHub and make sure the Root folder (the very first page of your repo) does not have a package.json or vercel.json anymore.

They should only exist inside the frontend folder.

If Vercel sees two package.json files, it gets Error 127 because it doesn't know which one to "boot" from.

If it still says 127:
It means the Root Directory setting we talked about earlier didn't "stick."

Use Desktop Mode on your phone.

Go to Settings > General.

Ensure Root Directory says frontend.

Ensure Framework Preset says Vite.

Look for the Save button at the very bottom of the page. It is often a blue bar that pops up. If you don't tap that, Vercel keeps trying to build your empty main folder!

Once you hit Save, go to Deployments and hit Redeploy. Switching to the "Vite" preset usually clears out all these weird path errors instantly!

