### The "Clean" Template
If you want to be 100% sure, try to make your `package.json` structure look exactly like this:

```json
{
  "name": "bike-km-tracker",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "private": true,
  "dependencies": {
    "react": "^18.3.1",
    "react-dom": "^18.3.1",
    "lucide-react": "^0.454.0",
    "recharts": "^2.13.0",
    "vite": "^5.4.10"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "dev": "vite",
    "build": "vite build --outDir dist",
    "preview": "vite preview"
  },
  "devDependencies": {
    "@eslint/js": "9.23.0",
    "autoprefixer": "^10.4.20",
    "postcss": "^8.4.49",
    "tailwindcss": "^3.4.17"
  }
}
Pro Tip: Copy your entire package.json text and paste it into a site like JSONLint.com. It will highlight the exact line where the "unexpected character" is hiding.

Once you fix that character, push it to GitHub. We are so close to seeing those Laakkwartier and Escamp totals on a live screen!

it looks like:  "dependencies": {

    "vite": "^5.0.0",

    "react": "^18.0.0",

    "react-dom": "^18.0.0",

    "@hookform/resolvers": "^5.0.1",

That's likely where the error is hiding. If that's the end of your dependencies block, that trailing comma after "^5.0.1" is exactly what is causing the Unexpected non-whitespace character error.

JSON is very strict: the last item in any section must not have a comma after it.

How to fix it:
Remove the comma after the last version number in that list.

Close the bracket.

It should look like this:

JSON
  "dependencies": {
    "vite": "^5.0.0",
    "react": "^18.0.0",
    "react-dom": "^18.0.0",
    "@hookform/resolvers": "^5.0.1" // No comma here!
  },
The PC Quick-Check:
Since you are on your PC, look for any red squiggly lines in your code editor (like VS Code). If the editor shows a red line at the end of that block, it's confirming that the extra comma is breaking the file.

Once that comma is gone and the file is saved:

Push to GitHub.

Vercel will automatically try to build it again.

With vite now inside the dependencies list, the "command not found" error should finally disappear.

You're at the very last step. Fix that comma, and you'll be able to see your distance logs for Laakkwartier (8.6km), Escamp (5.6km), and the rest of your routes live on the web.

it doesnt end after the hookform line, so there is a comma because it keeps going

That makes total sense—if there are more lines after @hookform/resolvers, the comma belongs there. The "Unexpected character" error usually means the JSON "bridge" was broken somewhere else nearby.

Since you are on your PC, let’s use a quick "Search and Destroy" method to find that stray character.

Where to look for the error:
The "Ghost" Character: Check if there is a random letter, dot, or symbol outside of the curly braces { } at the very top or very bottom of the file.

The Middle Bridge: Check the transition between sections. It should look like this:

JSON
"dependencies": {
    "item": "version",
    "last-item": "version"
},  <-- Check this comma! It must be between the } and the next "section"
"scripts": {
    ...
}
Quotes: Ensure every package name and version is inside double quotes ". JSON will crash if you use single quotes ' or forget one.

