By using this clean template, you're doing three things at once:

Fixing the "Unexpected Character" syntax error.

Ensuring vite is in the dependencies list so Vercel can find it.

Keeping your hookform and other tools intact.

Save it, push it to GitHub, and let's see Vercel finally turn green. You've put in a lot of work since last night—seeing that live link and being able to log your 18.6km Leidschenveen and 16km Loosduinen rides will be a huge win!

since vercel mentioned my position, can i look that position up in my github code?

Yes, you absolutely can! Since you are on your PC, finding "Position 2169" is much easier than it would be on a phone. That number refers to the character count from the very start of the file.

Here are the best ways to hunt it down:

1. Use "Go to Line" in VS Code
Most code editors don't show "character position" by default, but they show Line and Column in the bottom right corner.

Open your package.json.

Look at the bottom right status bar in VS Code. It will say something like Ln 50, Col 12.

As you move your cursor toward the end of the file, that "Col" (Column) count represents the position.

Shortcut: In many editors, if you look at the very bottom status bar, it will show the total length of the file. If 2169 is near the end, check the very last few lines for a stray symbol.

2. The "Search" Trick
Since the error is an "Unexpected non-whitespace character," it usually means something is sitting where it doesn't belong (like a random letter or a second closing brace).

Press Ctrl + F (Find).

Search for }} (double closing braces). You should only have one set at the very end.

Check for any characters after the final }. The file must end with that bracket and nothing else.

3. Use an Online Validator (Fastest)
The most foolproof way to see exactly where you messed up:

Copy your entire package.json code.

Go to JSONLint.

Paste your code and hit Validate JSON.

It will give you an error message like Error: Parse error on line 64: ... and highlight the exact spot in red.

Why this happened
Vercel is seeing your code as one long string of text. Position 2169 is the 2,169th character. Usually, this error pops up because:

You have a comma after the very last item in the file.

You have two } at the end instead of one.

You accidentally typed a letter (like v or k) outside of the brackets while saving.

Fix that one character, push to GitHub, and let's get those bike routes live! Having those 8.6km (Laakkwartier) and 5.6km (Escamp) distances ready to log is the best motivation to squash this bug.

